  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 351 PM EDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\n===== Monday - Tuesday ======\n\nForecast uncertainty is a concern for the first half of the upcoming \nweek due to weak forcing and potential MCS activity, which is often \nhandled poorly by deterministic guidance. Additionally, the \ntemperature forecast for the time frame could be derailed if we do \nsee thunderstorm activity, so there are multiple conditional \nforecast parameters at play for the beginning of the long term \nperiod. \n\nStrong upper ridging will continue to have a tight hold over the \nwestern US, with downstream troughing across the eastern US, which \nplaces the Ohio Valley in deep NW flow aloft. A more amplified \nshortwave looks to pivot through the NW flow Monday and Tuesday, \nwhich will likely trigger some thunderstorm development across the \nGreat Lakes and Upper Midwest region. However, due to the \norientation of the steering flow aloft, will expect any thunderstorm \ncomplexes that develop will take a southeast dive into the lower \nOhio Valley. There remains considerable uncertainty regarding if the \nforecast areas would even be impacted by these passing complexes, \nbut SPC does include a Marginal and Slight risk for Monday across \nmajority of the area. Both the GFS and ECMWF indicate a second MCS \ncluster moving in from the north late Monday morning, which would \nultimately arrive before the peak of daytime heating and greatest \ndestabilization. If that would happen to play out, it would most \nlikely derail the temperature forecast for Monday afternoon, which \ncurrently has highs in the mid 90s and heat indices over 100F. If \nthat MCS does not occur in our area, then there is high confidence \nthat a Heat Advisory would be needed as we should see heat indices \nreach the 103-110F range. \n\nMajority of the thunderstorm activity is expected remain to our \nnorth through this time frame due to the mean 700-300mb flow, though \nour north and northeast sections of the forecast area will be the \nmost susceptible to being clipped by passing complexes. As mentioned \nin the previous discussion, we typically still like where SPC drew \nthe outlook for Monday, but continue to advertise this being a \nconditional threat. \n\nIf we end up staying mostly dry for Monday and Tuesday, forecast \ntemps could be in the mid 90s again for Tuesday, and possibly \nanother day of Heat Advisory apparent temps. This is also \nconditional on how the thunderstorm chances play out. \n\n===== Late Next Week =====\n\nAn upper shortwave looks to deepen into a low over the Northeast for \nWednesday and into late next week, which will likely lead to pulling \nany substantial moisture off to the east and leaving us dry for \nWednesday and Thursday. Temps also may decrease slightly back into \nthe 80s for this time. There are signs that the upper ridge may \nfinally begin to weaken by late next week, though we remain in \ngeneral NW flow aloft all of next week.",
